WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.960
Let's get this show on the road.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:11.400
Okay.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.280
We have white against an 1890.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.200
We did play the Jabobba London, uh, in one game that's up on YouTube, but

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.400
now we're back to the official recommendations, E4, E5.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:30.120
So when we were like 14, 1500, we were playing mostly the Vienna, just to

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:34.660
remind you, my two major recommendations for beginner and intermediate players

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.560
are the Vienna, which goes Knight C3, Knight F6, and F4, as well as the

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:43.280
Four Knights Scotch, which is Knight F3, Knight C6, Knight C3, Knight F6.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.160
That's the four, four Knights.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.360
And then the Scotch part consists of the move D4.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.360
So I'm basically eeny, meeny, miny, moeing it.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:56.040
Um, let me think about what I want to play in this particular game.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.240
You could play the Vienna.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.280
Obviously the Vienna loses some of its luster at a higher level, uh, because

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.240
people know the theory and most people don't fall for some of the basic traps

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.560
that can give White a winning position.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:12.240
Uh, so let's go for our other big weapon and let's go Four Knights Scotch.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.160
Don't remember if we've had a Four Knights Scotch game in this speed run.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.520
You can probably look that up pretty easily, but that doesn't really matter.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.360
So of course, in this position, Black is not obligated to play Knight F6, but the

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.800
other moves contain pretty serious drawbacks.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:27.160
Okay.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:33.440
F5 is a move that I've seen tangentially, but never really analyzed.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.280
This is the deferred Latvian Gambit.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.160
So the Latvian Gambit, which we actually just talked about for those watching on

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:44.480
YouTube, Latvian Gambit came up in one of our discussions after Knight F3, F5

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.440
signifies the traditional Latvian Gambit.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.000
The insertion of the moves, Knight C6, Knight C3 changes the nature of the

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.480
position because Knight takes C5 is no longer possible.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:00.480
And in response to F5, White's main weapon is Knight takes C5.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.360
Latvian Gambit is essentially refuted.

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.320
Here, the E5 pawn is protected.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:09.760
Now, what is generally the approach when we, when we meet moves like F5?

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:10.480
All right.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.480
Moves like F5 are often considered unsound because they make permanent and

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.160
tremendous weaknesses on the King side.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.440
And generally the best way to exploit that is to open up the center.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.360
So I think a lot of people, when seeing this move for the first time, would be

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:30.000
tempted to play a move like D3, which is of course very passive.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.160
It closes the Bishop and it justifies Black's setup.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.800
After D3, Knight F6, Black gets a great position.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.680
So of course we can accept the Gambit with E takes F5, but that affords Black a

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:47.320
valuable tempo that they can use for development, for example, Knight F6.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.400
And of course, it also justifies the move F5.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:55.600
You can almost think of it as a reverse King's Gambit where taking this pawn

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.160
isn't quite as good when the White Queen doesn't have a path to H5.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.200
And we can't support the pawn with G4 because the Knight on F3 blocks the

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.040
Queen's access to these two squares.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:11.280
So what I'm getting at is that there's only one move, which is calling

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.480
my name in this position, and it should be calling your name as well.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.480
And you should be playing it really without all that much calculation,

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.560
because there really isn't that much to calculate.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.240
This move does not come with a big risk tag, right?

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:26.640
You play D4, we're not sacrificing anything.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.480
We are tearing the center apart in order to open up avenues

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.840
toward to get to Black's King.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.240
It might seem like this move is very complicated and you need to think about

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.160
it, but if you break down what's actually happening in the center, you'll see

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.600
that it's pretty simple to follow.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.480
E takes D4, we just play Knight takes D4.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.840
Black can take twice on D4, but then our Queen comes out, and the

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.600
Queen on D4 is going to be a monster.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.400
If Black takes the other pawn, F takes E4, that's a little bit trickier there.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.640
We have to be careful because if we recapture on E4, then we allow Black

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.840
to move D5 with tempo, and Black can drive a wedge into the center with E4.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:08.480
So in response to F takes E4, we should actually then take the pawn on E5,

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.640
offering a Knight trade, and in the resulting position, not only is Black's

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.640
King in mortal danger, but we also have a big development advantage, and Black

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.520
will just have a hard time keeping the E4 pawn protected.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:21.920
This is going to be really, really fun.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:25.040
I'm looking forward to this, and it's pretty encouraging that our opponent

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.240
is taking his time because that means this isn't some deep gambit preparation.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:35.200
But hopefully you're able to understand the logical underpinnings of the move D4,

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.920
and you're able to see that this move can be played largely on the basis of intuition.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.320
What am I expecting Black to do here?

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.720
I think our opponent is likely to play E takes D4.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:47.840
Is Black lost out of the opening here?

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.800
Actually, the answer to that question might be yes.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.880
From a practical perspective, handling the resulting complications when the King is

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:57.920
as weak as it is, is virtually impossible.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.800
I think White is probably already borderline winning, barring some sort of

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:10.320
computer play.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.320
Yeah, not GG just yet.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.320
I'm very curious how our opponent responds to this move.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.800
It's crazy that four moves in, this much could already be happening.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:30.080
The move F5 is just so incredibly damaging because it opens up so many new pathways

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.760
to the Black King. Queen h5 will always come with check.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.160
If we bring the bishop out to c4, the King is not going to be able to castle.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.400
Those are the two main drawbacks of pushing the f-pawn this early in the opening.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.600
Okay, so E takes d4, of course we play knight takes d4.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.000
Note that in response to knight takes knight, we shouldn't give a check on h5.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.720
This check is not currently all that dangerous because it can be blocked with g6.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:02.320
Your mental reference should be, okay, if there's a knight, let's say on e5 or h4,

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:07.920
then g6 can be met with knight takes g6, and that's a classic trick that hopefully

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.720
you've seen in puzzle rushes and in different kinds of puzzles, even if you're a beginner.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.760
Here, queen h5 is not an immediate threat.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:20.880
If it was our move, well, obviously the f5 pawn is currently hanging, but assuming it

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.840
wasn't, our next move would be bishop c4 and then castles, and hopefully the e5

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:28.560
opens just in time for us to put a rook on e1.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.320
The most important factor is going to be preventing black from castling kingside.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.240
We have to do whatever it takes to prevent black from castling kingside.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:40.480
Okay, this is a very juicy position.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:47.440
So, again, the instinct is to just recapture on e4, but when you're trying to punish

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:52.800
a gambit or an unsound opening, you have to think very concretely, and you always

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.160
have to play for maximum.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.760
So just meekly recapturing a pawn without considering the alternatives is the best way

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.440
to justify the gambit or the unsound line.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:08.640
And there's one trick that I'm spotting here, which I think comes very close to winning

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:10.800
this game for white right off the bat.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.160
Now, if you know anything about openings that involve an early push of the f pawn, for example,

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:20.320
there's the Damiano, which might be the most famous of these unsound defenses.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:22.400
It's f6 in this position.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:28.560
In this position, who can tell me what the classic refutation is to the move f6?

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.480
This is a move everybody should know.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.440
It's actually not the only way to refute f6, but it's the most famous.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:41.680
You sacrifice the knight on e5, and in response to f takes e5, you deliver a check on h5.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.840
Didn't I just say that the check isn't dangerous?

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:48.480
Well, there's another circumstance under which queen h5 wins the game, and that is

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:53.760
if you can take on e5, not only is that a check, that forks the rook in the corner.

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.200
Queen picks up the rook, and then the queen easily gets out of the corner because black

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.520
doesn't have the machinery to attack it with.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.920
I'll show this in some depth after the game.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:06.000
What similarity can we extract from the Damiano?

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:12.240
Well, the only big similarity is that the f pawn has moved out of its initial square,

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.240
allowing us to check on h5.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:18.640
But there's a second similarity, which is that the e5 square is now accessible to the

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:22.880
white queen once the f pawn has moved out of f5.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:27.520
Not immediately, because the knight is guarding that square, but we can trade the knight.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.600
So I think knight takes c6 essentially wins the game.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:37.840
As crazy as it may seem to trade pieces when you're trying to punish an unsound opening,

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.160
this is what I meant when I said you have to think concretely, right?

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:47.360
Knight c6 isn't necessarily a logical move, trading a knight in the center for a knight

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:51.120
that has just come out, but it's a move based on concrete considerations.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:57.440
Black has to recapture, then we deliver a check on h5, g6 gets forked.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:08:59.040
Okay, this doesn't change anything.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.360
We still check on h5.

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:07.280
In fact, dc I think is the only way for black to stay alive for longer than a couple of

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:07.520
moves.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:09.600
Yeah, this is what black has to do, king e7.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.840
Obviously, our opening strategy has resulted in success, but it's important not to rest

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:16.640
on our laurels.

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:22.560
Actually winning these types of positions where you have what seems to be a decisive

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:25.920
development advantage can be a lot harder and more frustrating than it appears.

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.680
So don't think that our job is already done and everything wins.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:31.360
That could not be further from the truth.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.760
First things first, bishop g5 does not win the queen because black brings the knight

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:36.480
out to f6.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.040
Okay, so bishop g5 check might be a good move, but we shouldn't hope to win the queen of

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:41.600
that move.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:48.000
Now, after knight f6, I see the move knight takes e4, using the pin against the knight

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.160
and putting more pressure on the knight.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.200
So that's one way to play.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:54.880
We, of course, can still give the check on e5.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:01.200
That'll probably force black to play bishop e6 because the moment the king moves to f7,

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:05.360
the other bishop comes out with check, and that seems utterly devastating.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:06.560
So let's consider that for a second.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:09.280
Queen e5 check, bishop to e6.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:15.280
The bishop is pinned, so bishop c4 comes to mind in that position, but the queen can come

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:18.880
up and protect the bishop, simultaneously offering a queen trade.

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:22.960
That is the last thing that we want here, a queen trade, unless it is accompanied by,

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:24.800
let's say, material gain of some sort.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:29.440
Okay, so let me think about this for just a couple more seconds.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.760
I have a pretty cool idea in mind.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:37.360
I'm just verifying that it's as good as it looks.

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:39.520
I really think it is.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:41.040
I think it is.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:48.000
Okay, so here's how I propose that we continue.

00:10:48.720 --> 00:10:51.760
I still like the move bishop g5 check, for starters.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:53.680
I also like queen e5 check.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:57.120
I think there are multiple ways to reach a decisive advantage.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.440
Even the simple knight takes e4.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.920
I talked about this a couple of games ago when we had a comparable attack.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:08.800
I think it came from an elephant, where oftentimes there's a temptation to overcomplicate and

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:13.440
start calculating long lines, whereas a very simple move like knight takes e4 keeps all

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:14.960
of the advantages of the position.

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.680
It restores material equality, and it gets a knight to the center.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:24.560
So if you're struggling and you're not finding anything, resorting to simple moves such as

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:29.200
a recapture or a developing move can be an easy way to maintain the pressure.

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:34.640
So I'd like to start either with bishop g5 check or with bishop c4.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.640
I don't actually think it matters.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:42.000
Yeah, let's start, hmm, let me think about this.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.240
Okay, let's go bishop c4.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.280
So we're bringing out the light-squared bishop.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.640
We're threatening immediate checkmate on f7.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.920
And I am hoping for the move queen to e8.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:55.120
This is what I'm expecting.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:57.440
Why am I expecting queen e8?

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:00.560
Well, black somehow has to defend the f7 square.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.680
We've already established that g6 never works because of queen e5 check, at the very least

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:06.880
picking up the rook in the corner.

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:13.360
Knight g8 to h6 walks into bishop g5, and black has nothing to block the pin with.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:18.960
The only other move that I see here is bishop c8 to e6, intercepting our bishop

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:20.800
but I didn't take that move too seriously.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:25.440
Maybe I should have, but I figured even if we trade on e6, that brings the black king

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.720
out even to an even more vulnerable spot.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:31.520
And in that resulting position, again, we can play it simple.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:36.480
We can play knight takes e4, for goodness sake, and then the knight jumps into g5.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:38.320
That has to be devastating.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:42.560
The greatest thing about playing this position is that we're not down material.

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:47.680
It's not like we've sacrificed anything to reach this position, and that is why you

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:54.400
can allow yourself to play in simpler ways than you would have been able to if you were

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.960
down a piece right now, and you really had something to prove.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:01.600
Here, you can just kind of chill out and play simple moves, and worse comes to worse.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:06.640
Okay, your opponent consolidates, you reset, and you continue attacking.

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:08.560
Let's take on e6.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:12.960
We're playing this like a Russian schoolboy, trading off one of black's only defenders.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:17.600
Now the king is all alone, but we don't have the machinery to execute this move.

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:19.680
To exploit the king position yet.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.280
All we have is our queen currently in the attack.

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.360
We can give, let's say, a check on g4, but that's counterproductive because it sends

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.360
the king back to safety on f7.

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:33.920
The queen is very well placed here because it's blocking off the king's escape back to

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:35.280
f7, back to the king's side.

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:37.280
Let's play knight takes e4.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:40.880
Why didn't I instead develop the bishop?

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.280
Because I was worried about knight g8 to f6.

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:48.160
And once the queen is knocked off of h5, the king can drop back to f7.

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:51.280
And once it reaches f7, black comes very close to consolidating.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:56.000
All black will have to do then is bring their bishop out, move the rook, and kind of manually

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:56.960
castle.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:13:59.760
The king will eventually reach the promised land on g8.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:03.680
So that's what we have to avoid at all costs, and that's why I took on e4.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:05.840
I'm threatening to deliver a check on g5.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.760
Forcing the king in the other direction, which is what we want.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.440
If we can force the king onto the d-file, hopefully you're able to see that the rook

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:17.360
on h1 is only one tempo away from joining the attack.

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:21.920
I mean, we will castle king side at some point, and then we'll be ready to play rook d1.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:27.200
Queen also defends that square, so we can play rook d1 even if the black king hasn't

00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:28.080
blocked the queen.

00:14:29.040 --> 00:14:31.360
So knight g5 is one huge threat.

00:14:31.360 --> 00:14:36.400
Don't forget that knight f7 could be a simple way to win material, regardless of the king's

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:36.880
placement.

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:41.440
Of course, black could make a mad dash for the queen side, king d7, castles king c8.

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:46.000
But once the king reaches c8, it doesn't, you know, black's problems aren't solved there,

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.280
and I think we can still get to the king if it's on the queen side.

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:55.040
If he goes queen to e8, well, obviously then we avoid the queen's trade.

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:56.880
I'd have to think about it.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.800
Queen e8 is probably black's best move.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.680
I think we can deliver a check on g4 in that case, and that contradicts what I just said

00:15:05.680 --> 00:15:07.520
– it allows the king to move back to f7.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:10.240
We really have no choice in that situation.

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:16.320
But the caveat is that after we castle, our knight still is able to jump into g5.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:22.080
So the king on f7 isn't fully safe if we're able to deliver the check on g5, and I'll

00:15:22.080 --> 00:15:23.680
show some of those lines after the game.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:25.760
Queen d5 is definitely a good move.

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:26.720
It's a good defensive move.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:29.840
Now we rule out queen takes d5 check.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:32.480
The obvious move, I think, is knight to g5 check.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:40.160
That forces the king back toward d7 or e7, and in all cases, I think the king doesn't

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:44.560
reach safety because there's always these diagonal checks that we can deliver if the

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:45.680
king moves to d7.

00:15:45.680 --> 00:15:50.800
If the king moves to e7, then we'll most certainly castle and put a rook on e1 and

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:54.560
continue shepherding the king toward the most dangerous part of the board, which is the

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:55.120
d-file.

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:00.320
I don't see a reason for us to dilly-dally.

00:16:00.320 --> 00:16:02.640
I think we should give this check promptly.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:04.240
Not over.

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:10.400
I mean, our opponent is defending resiliently, and if you play an engine in a position like

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:16.000
this, you're going to lose like 99 times out of 100 because actually finishing someone

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:20.160
off who's defending super accurately—Hikaru does this too—you can feel like you're

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:25.200
mating him 10 different ways, but checkmating the king in an open board when the king is

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:29.360
stuck in the center like this is incredibly hard because the king has so many directions

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:34.160
that it can escape to, and the center is open, which means that there's a high probability

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:39.440
and possibility of trades, so you've got to manage all of that load and hunt the king

00:16:39.440 --> 00:16:41.600
at the same time with limited material.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:47.360
That's hard, so don't feel like the checkmate in these types of king hunts is easy or you're

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:50.720
missing something obvious if you don't see something convincing.

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:53.920
So we're waiting for our opponent to decide where he wants to put the king.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:59.040
I'm expecting king d7, but the best move is probably king to e7.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.360
In both cases, I think we're just going to castle short without too much of a thought.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.160
Without a second thought, I should say.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:08.480
We've already put in some thought.

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:17.040
Man, this is completely winning, no doubt about it, but a couple more precise moves

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:18.080
are required.

00:17:19.360 --> 00:17:22.480
I think some people watching this thus far might be disappointed that we haven't sacked

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:24.560
or might be disappointed that we haven't sacrificed anything.

00:17:24.560 --> 00:17:26.720
We haven't made any sexy moves.

00:17:26.720 --> 00:17:29.840
We've just literally played the most simple moves on the board.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:32.080
We made a developing move.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:33.840
We made a capture.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:39.840
We made a capture, and then we delivered the most obvious check, and this comes back to

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:44.400
the point I made in that Aleppan game, which ultimately resulted in checkmate, which is

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:50.960
that I think when you read books on attacking and tactics, the art of attack in chess might

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:56.880
be misrepresented in your mind, and you might reach the false idea that every attack somehow

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:00.720
culminates in a combination or must feature some sort of a sacrifice.

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:07.520
In fact, 90% to 95% of attacks in chess are attacks of the salt-of-the-earth variety,

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:12.640
where you just make a bunch of simple attacking moves, and the king is checkmated, or you

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:17.600
win a decisive amount of material, and it's important to understand that because then

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:21.040
you kind of know what you're looking for, and you shouldn't shy away from these straightforward

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:21.680
moves.

00:18:21.680 --> 00:18:23.440
Thank you, ChessDojo, for the raid.

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:24.080
Appreciate it.

00:18:24.880 --> 00:18:30.480
Not trying to, you know, get on the soapbox here, but I think I know quite a few players

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:35.120
who tend to rule out certain moves because they're quote-unquote too simple or they're

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:36.240
not sacrificial enough.

00:18:36.240 --> 00:18:41.680
There's a time for sacrifice, but that time doesn't occur as frequently as you might think

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:42.480
when you're attacking.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:44.800
Can Magnus hold this position?

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:45.360
I don't think so.

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.960
In a classical game, I think this is borderline resignable for black.

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:51.440
Somebody was proposing Kf6 here.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:55.520
It doesn't fundamentally change the fabric of the position.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:56.960
I still think that we should castle.

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:00.240
Why is it so important to castle as Kf6 is played?

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.800
Because Rook e8 check is a potential idea.

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:08.960
Although, on second thought, that square is protected, so maybe we can use this tempo

00:19:08.960 --> 00:19:10.480
to do something a little bit smarter.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:15.840
I have a second idea that I just came up with, which is a move I didn't really appreciate

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:19.280
at first, but the more I look at it, the more I like it.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:23.520
I will admit that I didn't notice the e8 square was defended until just a second ago.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:30.400
So I think a lot of people are catching my drift here, which is that the bishop would

00:19:30.400 --> 00:19:33.760
be ideally placed on the long diagonal.

00:19:33.760 --> 00:19:39.760
And so a lot of people come up with the move b3, but you really have to optimize your ideas

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:40.800
in this type of position.

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:44.080
Don't just make the first execution of the idea that you see.

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:51.760
Try to weigh its minuses and act on those minuses to find a different, better execution.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.280
What is the drawback of b3?

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:57.920
Well, it's not actually a developing move, and it allows black to deliver a very unpleasant

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:59.120
check on b4.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:04.480
Then black can beat white to the punch and stick a bishop on c3, which prevents us from

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:05.440
playing bishop b2.

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:08.560
So what's the different execution that I'm speaking of?

00:20:08.560 --> 00:20:13.360
Well, it's the simple bishop to d2, the modest-looking bishop to d2.

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:14.960
We're aiming for c3.

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:19.280
Once the bishop reaches c3, I feel like the king is just busted.

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:24.880
We force the king back to the e-file, then we can castle, and then once a rook lands

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:28.800
on e1, literally every piece will be joining in the attack.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:33.600
So I don't see any reason for us not to play bishop d2.

00:20:33.600 --> 00:20:39.920
Notice that queen e5 check might be a scary-looking move because it forces white's king to move,

00:20:39.920 --> 00:20:41.600
but we don't care about that.

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:42.960
All we need is one rook.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:45.680
We don't really need both of our rooks in the attack.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:51.360
So after queen e5, king f1, that's not the rook that we expected would be jumping in,

00:20:51.360 --> 00:20:52.160
but who cares?

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:54.880
Rook a1 to e1 is going to be devastating.

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:57.600
Queen f7 will be a mate threat in that resulting position.

00:20:58.240 --> 00:21:00.000
I call this thinking in terms of squares.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:04.800
Anytime you're attacking a wide-open king like this, you at all times have to be aware

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:07.600
of the potential mating constructions.

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:11.760
In this case, I only really see one, which is queen f7, but you make a mental note to

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:16.400
yourself that anytime black's queen loses contact with f7, you're going to be aware

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:19.360
that queen f7 becomes a serious idea.

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:24.160
I don't see any other mating constructions with the black king on f6.

00:21:25.200 --> 00:21:27.760
Okay, g6, g6.

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:29.280
Well, that doesn't change anything.

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:33.040
We, of course, still play bishop c3 check, and I think this is going to lead to decisive

00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:34.320
material loss imminently.

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:42.640
Note, by the way, that the discovery, knight takes h7 check, is possible but clearly inferior

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:46.320
because the king is able to reach g7.

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:50.720
Black can also sacrifice the exchange on h7, and the black queen is defended.

00:21:50.720 --> 00:21:52.400
It's defended by the c6 pawn.

00:21:52.400 --> 00:21:58.080
So knight takes h7 here is ill-advised, and it certainly does not win the queen.

00:21:58.080 --> 00:22:01.840
Of course, we play bishop c3 check anyway, forcing the king toward the center.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:07.120
And after the king reaches the center, you should, of course, also notice that the rook

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:12.000
on h8 is hanging, which might not seem significant, but it is, right?

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:15.040
When you're attacking, you're not just hunting for checkmate.

00:22:15.040 --> 00:22:18.800
You're also trying to win a decisive amount of material.

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:24.960
That is a totally legitimate outcome of an attack, simplifying and emerging, let's say,

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.360
up a piece or up a rook in an endgame.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:32.560
That can be a practical and simple way to finish off an attack and convert it into something

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:33.120
tangible.

00:22:33.920 --> 00:22:35.120
Yeah, very good, queen e2.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:40.560
Queen e2 check is going to finish the game once the king reaches e7 because the king

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.600
is going to be forced back to the d-file where we will pick it off.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:46.640
Well, rather, we'll pick the queen off with rook to d1.

00:22:46.640 --> 00:22:48.560
And that is all she wrote.

00:22:49.280 --> 00:22:52.000
King cannot move to f6, cannot move to f7.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:56.080
Okay, so there are a couple of modifications that we can make here.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:58.560
But again, let's keep it as simple as possible.

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:04.400
Of course, you should see that black can play rook e8, but that's what I was talking about.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:07.440
In that position, we can, if we want, keep our queen.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:12.720
We can play rook takes queen and then intercept the e-file with bishop e5, but there is a

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:16.480
much simpler way to guarantee victory after rook e8.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:17.760
Much simpler.

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:19.520
Who can spot this sequence of moves?

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:24.400
The move order actually doesn't really matter, but you want to try to follow the most clinical,

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:25.840
the most forcing move order.

00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:28.400
Yeah, you could even play bishop takes h8 here.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:31.040
That is a way to really rub it in black's face, who the boss is.

00:23:31.040 --> 00:23:36.400
But much simpler is to first take the queen, then take the rook, and then take the other

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:36.720
rook.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:41.840
Or we can immediately play, actually, we can immediately play bishop takes h8, rook takes

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.320
e2, king takes e2, and we are up a full rook.

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:49.120
Once you see a continuation like this that you know for a fact is going to guarantee

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:53.600
victory, as it just did, there's really no need for us to search for anything else.

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:57.840
There's no need for us to calculate a move like bishop e5, maybe black has this.

00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:00.320
This is exactly how you butcher these types of games.

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:07.680
You try to get too fancy, and you try to checkmate rather than make the most practical

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:08.800
conversion of the attack.

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:10.160
That was nice.

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:17.520
It was a simple game, but it really illustrates how bad the move f5 is across most openings,

00:24:17.520 --> 00:24:19.200
with the exception, of course, of the dutch.

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:26.000
But the reason f5 here is justified is because this diagonal is closed, and this diagonal

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:26.640
is closed, right?

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:31.760
White hasn't moved the e-pawn yet, so it really changes the dynamic of the game.

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:36.000
Although f5 is a pretty dubious opening, even against 1d4.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:41.920
You have to know a lot of very tactical lines, like the Staunton Gambit with e4 is nothing

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:48.240
to sneeze at, and this can get very, very, very dicey for black if you don't know all

00:24:48.240 --> 00:24:48.800
of the theory.

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:55.360
So let's have a look at exactly how bad the move f5 here is.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:57.840
So we respond with d4.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:25:00.720
Hopefully, the logic behind this move was clear enough.

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:04.960
Really, really quickly, I was referencing the Damiano, which is f6.

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:12.640
There is a fascinating story between why the Damiano is called what it is called, right?

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:18.000
You might say, well, what kind of an idiot was Damiano to advocate for a move like f6,

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:23.600
which even if the year is 1400 or 1500, which is when Damiano lived, and opening theory

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:28.880
isn't well developed, just using first principles, it's obvious that f6 is a very bad move.

00:25:28.880 --> 00:25:32.080
You're blocking the knight's normal developing square.

00:25:32.080 --> 00:25:33.760
You're opening up a diagonal.

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:36.080
You're weakening the kingside permanently.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:41.200
So it's obviously a bad move, and even d4 leads to an almost decisive advantage for

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:41.440
white.

00:25:41.440 --> 00:25:43.600
But knight takes e5 is the famous refutation.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:50.640
It's been known for hundreds of years, really since the 1500s, and it is followed by queen

00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:53.280
h5 check, forking the king in the pawn.

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:55.040
One fork turns into another.

00:25:55.040 --> 00:25:57.200
If black plays g6, we win the rook in the corner.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:26:03.680
It's important to point out that in this position, black cannot play bishop g7 on the next move

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:05.040
because of the pin against the bishop.

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:10.800
So I think the simplest is to play d3, and essentially to pre-move bishop g5, which pins

00:26:10.800 --> 00:26:12.320
and potentially wins the knight.

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:14.320
Black loses all of his frickin' pieces.

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:19.760
Or you can even play bishop to h6 if you want to keep it simple, and then simplify by taking

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:22.240
on f8 with the queen and forcing a queen trade.

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:26.000
White is up an exchange and a pawn in the resulting endgame.

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:26.880
There are many ways to win.

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:29.600
You can analyze this with an engine if you want to be very diligent.

00:26:29.600 --> 00:26:30.800
You can also play knight c3.

00:26:30.800 --> 00:26:37.520
Okay, so g6 loses, and king e7 loses in even more spectacular fashion because the king

00:26:37.520 --> 00:26:42.080
has driven to the edge of the earth with a series of checks, and king g6 gets checkmated

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:43.600
in a very famous style.

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:45.040
This is a very famous line.

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:46.400
Again, it's been known for centuries.

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:51.760
Check, now a discovered check with a pawn, and now the classic, right?

00:26:51.760 --> 00:26:52.480
You can see the rook.

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:53.280
You can see the king.

00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:54.400
They are x-ringing each other.

00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:58.000
So h4 is a very thematic mating move.

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:00.320
You're threatening hg and queen f7.

00:27:00.320 --> 00:27:04.080
It's mate in nine according to the engine if black gives up like all of his pieces.

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:06.800
So white can play bishop takes g5 or hg.

00:27:06.800 --> 00:27:08.160
There's nothing left to analyze.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:15.440
Technically, it is more resilient to play d5 with the idea of protecting the f5 square

00:27:15.440 --> 00:27:18.320
with the bishop, and here white has many ways to win.

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:22.400
But there's a very beautiful line that also has been known for many centuries.

00:27:22.400 --> 00:27:29.280
It starts with h4, so you're threatening to play h5 followed by a discovery with d4,

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:30.800
forcing black to make some luft.

00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:34.400
And now there is a beautiful move that ends the game on the spot.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:36.720
It wins a decisive amount of material.

00:27:36.720 --> 00:27:38.320
Who knows what this move is?

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:42.080
And by the way, the same move would follow after h5.

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:43.680
It would come with even greater effect.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:45.600
It's bishop takes b7.

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:47.120
Yeah, this is worth knowing.

00:27:47.120 --> 00:27:48.000
This is worth knowing.

00:27:48.000 --> 00:27:51.920
It's a deflection sacrifice, but it's not really a sacrifice because this is checkmate.

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:58.160
And in the event of h5, this just takes a couple moves longer here, here.

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:04.000
And the clinical move here is actually queen to f7, threatening hg, and rook takes h5,

00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:04.640
which will be mate.

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:08.160
So this is definitely worth remembering.

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:10.720
What else is there to say?

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:16.880
I guess black has bishop d6, but as long as we maintain contact with the f5 square,

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:18.320
this move doesn't change anything.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:23.680
Queen to a5 is the computer move, which evades any further attacks on the queen.

00:28:23.680 --> 00:28:26.320
Black can play knight c6, but then we take c6.

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:30.000
We're up two pawns, and the raging attack against the king continues.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:33.520
So this is really all you have to know.

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:35.600
h4 followed by bishop takes b7.

00:28:35.600 --> 00:28:38.320
Now someone was asking what happens if black doesn't take the knight.

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:41.520
Yes, the best move for black here is the pathetic queen e7.

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:43.440
And this does win the pawn back.

00:28:43.440 --> 00:28:44.720
You just drop your knight back.

00:28:44.720 --> 00:28:48.000
But if black takes, look at the development advantage that white has.

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:50.560
I was going to say development advantage that white has developed.

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:54.880
White's going to go knight c3 castles, put something on e1, such as a rook,

00:28:54.880 --> 00:28:58.480
and black's queen is going to get absolutely massacred.

00:28:58.480 --> 00:29:01.520
This is plus three, according to the computer.

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:03.200
You can analyze this further on your own.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:06.160
It's been tried by some good players, but black is just dead lost here.

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:06.880
Dead lost.

00:29:07.520 --> 00:29:09.520
For example, let's say black plays knight c6.

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:11.280
I would actually castle, right?

00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:15.600
I wouldn't rush with knight c3 because the black queen is a great asset for white.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:20.880
If you play rookie one, either you win the queen, or you can deliver a devastating discover check

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:22.160
against the queen.

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:24.640
If the queen drops back, there's a bunch of cool moves.

00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:26.880
You can even play knight to e5.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:31.840
Chigorin actually fell for a trick like this the one time that he tried the Damiano,

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:33.600
and you lose the queen.

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:39.440
So wherever you look, there are sexy tactics, and this is really not worthy of analysis.

00:29:39.440 --> 00:29:42.160
But before we move to the game continuation,

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:49.680
the hilarious story behind why the Damiano is named after Damiano has to do with Ruy Lopez.

00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:51.920
Not the opening, but the man himself.

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:55.360
Ruy Lopez was a priest from Spain who lived in the 16th century.

00:29:55.360 --> 00:30:00.320
He wrote one of the first books on chess where he examined some openings,

00:30:00.320 --> 00:30:02.480
including the one that's named after him.

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:08.080
But a little known fact is that Ruy Lopez was actually the first person to...

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:11.760
I think he invented a couple of other openings.

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:16.400
He definitely considered the Italian, but believed that bishop b5 was a better move.

00:30:16.400 --> 00:30:18.560
That's why we name it Ruy Lopez.

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:22.400
He might have been the originator also of the Pansiani and a couple of other openings.

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:25.200
So his manuscript was really groundbreaking for the time.

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:28.880
The funny thing is that he had a feud with Damiano.

00:30:28.880 --> 00:30:31.440
I've talked about this on stream before.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:37.920
He had a flat-out feud with Damiano, who lived before him,

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:43.120
and published a manuscript before Ruy Lopez, which apparently Ruy Lopez was jealous about.

00:30:43.120 --> 00:30:47.600
So Ruy Lopez falsely attributed the move f6 to Damiano,

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:53.760
and basically called it the Damiano game, kind of as a troll, sarcastically.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.520
And it has stuck ever since.

00:30:56.800 --> 00:31:00.320
We think it's named after someone who advocated for this move,

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:03.600
but Damiano was actually the first one to refute f6.

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:06.400
He understood that f6 was bad.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:10.640
He gave the refutation in his book, and yet we still call it that.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:15.760
I have the actual evidence for that and the historical underpinnings of this

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:20.160
in a book that I have, but if I would rifle through it right now,

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:23.840
this YouTube video would take two hours, so I'll save that for another video.

00:31:25.040 --> 00:31:26.400
I'll save that for another video.

00:31:27.200 --> 00:31:32.560
Okay, so on we go to the game continuation, knight c3, f5, d4.

00:31:32.560 --> 00:31:38.000
Okay, e takes d4, I think, makes things even worse because it helps us open the center.

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:40.640
Black's position here is in dire straits.

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:46.640
According to the engine, f takes e4 is the only way to stay alive, more or less.

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:48.640
Knight takes e5 and knight f6.

00:31:48.640 --> 00:31:49.440
Here, black is…

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:54.160
Oh, no, black is actually busted because of a really cool line.

00:31:54.160 --> 00:32:01.760
So apparently, white is supposed to go here, which looks rather dumb because of d5.

00:32:01.760 --> 00:32:06.480
But black is so poorly developed that almost any sacrifices are going to be strong here.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:09.600
So for example, knight takes d5 is the engine move.

00:32:09.600 --> 00:32:13.600
Sacrificing a knight in order to pave the way for a check on h5.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:15.120
Now, what did I talk about during the game?

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:21.920
Anytime there's a knight on one of these three squares, g6 is innocuous because of knight takes g6.

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:25.600
And what's funny is that here you don't take the rook, you actually take the pawn.

00:32:26.320 --> 00:32:27.120
You take the pawn.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:31.120
If the king steps up to e7, you skewer the queen.

00:32:32.800 --> 00:32:38.000
So king d7 is forced and bishop takes d5 leads to clearly a decisive attack.

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:41.840
Now, this is Stockfish11 that I'm using on chess.com.

00:32:41.840 --> 00:32:45.600
You should probably confirm this with Stronger Engine, but it gives plus 4,

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:48.560
and I don't see any reason to doubt its evaluation.

00:32:48.560 --> 00:32:50.080
White's threatening checkmate in one.

00:32:50.960 --> 00:32:52.080
Let's say knight takes d4.

00:32:52.080 --> 00:32:53.280
Now the other bishop comes out.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:54.720
Bishop e6.

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:56.480
Oh, now a sexy move.

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:59.760
Bishop e6 check is winning.

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:05.200
Either black has to step into a discovery or black has to step into a skewer,

00:33:06.000 --> 00:33:07.520
and the bishop is pinned.

00:33:07.520 --> 00:33:08.880
So black can't block the check.

00:33:09.920 --> 00:33:14.720
Here you can even gobble up the pawn first, and only now you can take the queen.

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:16.720
Bishop e3 check.

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:21.920
White's got three additional pawns, c5, b4, with a decisive attack on the king,

00:33:21.920 --> 00:33:24.800
which has been driven all the way to the queen side, which I always kind of like.

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:30.560
Wow, that is a pretty juicy line.

00:33:31.760 --> 00:33:33.440
So where did that come from?

00:33:33.440 --> 00:33:37.280
Yeah, so f takes c4, knight f6 is more logical to me here.

00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:39.360
Bishop c4 is incredibly strong.

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:43.040
It threatens knight f7, so black's hand is essentially forced here.

00:33:43.040 --> 00:33:48.080
Knight takes d5, and if knight takes e5, then of course we just recapture,

00:33:48.080 --> 00:33:49.920
and now the queen defends the knight.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:53.200
And here I would even play like a Russian schoolboy and take back with a queen,

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:54.080
take back with a bishop.

00:33:54.080 --> 00:33:58.320
So these lines are pretty one-sided, but they're always very fun to look at,

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:01.760
because they really demonstrate the importance of developing your pieces,

00:34:01.760 --> 00:34:08.400
and the disaster that waits for he who pushes his f-pawn too early.

00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:10.880
Now you might be listening to this and saying, well, wait a second,

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:16.480
I learned that in the Ruy Lopez, knight f3, knight c6, bishop b5,

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:20.320
there is a variation called the Schliemann, where black plays f5.

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:24.560
You might have also learned from someone like Eric Rosen that f5 is a possible move

00:34:24.560 --> 00:34:28.080
even after white plays bishop c4, even in the Italian.

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:31.920
And how do you marry that with what I just said?

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:33.840
Is f5 a losing line?

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:35.200
Is the Schliemann refuted?

00:34:35.200 --> 00:34:36.640
No, the Schliemann is not refuted.

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:39.680
White is considered to be better in the Schliemann.

00:34:39.680 --> 00:34:42.720
Actually, the best move, ironically, is d3,

00:34:42.720 --> 00:34:46.560
which is the move that I criticized in the context of our game.

00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:53.040
But you have to remember that these things I say are very concretely tied into the position.

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:59.280
The bishop on b5 is not ideally placed in the context of punishing black from playing f5,

00:34:59.280 --> 00:35:03.440
because it's not controlling the a2-ga diagonal,

00:35:03.440 --> 00:35:05.920
which means black is going to have an easier time castling.

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:08.880
And if you play d4 in this position,

00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:13.600
the placement of the white bishop gives black a very important tempo.

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:17.680
So here, fe4, knight e5, knight e5, d5.

00:35:17.680 --> 00:35:20.000
This position could have occurred in our game

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:24.000
with the difference that there was a knight on c3, and the bishop was still on f1.

00:35:24.000 --> 00:35:29.440
Who can tell me how black can exploit the very awkward placement of the bishop

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:32.560
to win material and get out of the gates with an advantage?

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:34.240
Actually, black is better in this line.

00:35:35.280 --> 00:35:37.520
c6, yeah, black plays c6.

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:41.200
Okay, you might say, doesn't that send the bishop to where it wants to go anyway?

00:35:42.080 --> 00:35:44.320
Well, no, if you look carefully, this pawn is undefended.

00:35:44.320 --> 00:35:45.200
The king is on e1.

00:35:45.200 --> 00:35:47.840
Queen a5 check is a fork.

00:35:47.840 --> 00:35:51.760
And even though white has a development advantage, black will connect the pawns with d5.

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:56.320
And white is actually lost after d5, simply lost,

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:58.960
because this pawn chain is impregnable.

00:35:58.960 --> 00:36:01.520
And black is going to start getting his pieces out subsequently,

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:04.560
and black will not have a hard time castling.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:09.120
Now, you might say, okay, I get it, but what about it in the Italian?

00:36:09.120 --> 00:36:12.480
Is f5 possibly a move that black can get away with here?

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:14.160
And the answer is yes and no.

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:16.400
f5 is a borderline losing line.

00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:18.320
And I will have a YouTube video refuting it,

00:36:18.320 --> 00:36:21.680
because I know it's a popular subject of some YouTube videos.

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:25.600
The best move here is, in fact, to play d4, much like we did in the game.

00:36:26.240 --> 00:36:32.560
But after e takes d4, the chief difference is that if white plays knight takes d4,

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:36.560
kind of like we did in the game, I don't know what the mainline theory is,

00:36:36.560 --> 00:36:38.640
but I think the black move here is knight f6.

00:36:38.640 --> 00:36:41.040
I'm pretty sure this is the recommended move.

00:36:41.040 --> 00:36:44.000
And there's a very important concrete detail.

00:36:44.000 --> 00:36:48.720
The placement of the bishop on c4 is actually a drawback in this case.

00:36:49.280 --> 00:36:49.840
Why?

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:52.960
Because black has the move d5, hitting the bishop with tempo,

00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:57.280
and obviously the idea is to undermine the e4 pawn.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:37:00.160
So this is one of the lines which is often featured in

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:02.800
overviews of this variation, ed5, bishop f5.

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:06.080
White does not lose a piece because white recaptures,

00:37:06.080 --> 00:37:08.880
but in the ensuing endgame, black gets a huge initiative.

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:12.800
And this is exactly what the kind of position the gambit players want.

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:16.160
White might still be slightly better, but clearly we've gone astray.

00:37:16.880 --> 00:37:22.880
Now compare this to the situation after knight c3, f5, d4.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:26.960
If black plays knight f6 here, which probably he should still have done,

00:37:26.960 --> 00:37:29.440
I actually think this might be black's best move,

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:32.480
just sacrificing the pawn in order to get better development.

00:37:32.480 --> 00:37:35.360
The move d5, it doesn't come with tempo.

00:37:35.360 --> 00:37:36.880
It doesn't attack the bishop.

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:40.800
So I actually think that white could ignore everything

00:37:40.800 --> 00:37:44.880
that's happening in the center and play a move like bishop b5 to pin the knight.

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:49.360
And if black plays pawn takes pawn, yes, black wins the pawn back,

00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:52.320
but look at how crappy this endgame is for black.

00:37:52.320 --> 00:37:55.840
Now black's king is the one that's lost castling rights, e4 is weak,

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:59.920
and white can calmly castle kingside on the next move.

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:02.560
I know this is a lot of theory, but I'm really, I'm trying to give you

00:38:03.120 --> 00:38:05.120
the kind of foundation for the things that I say,

00:38:05.120 --> 00:38:08.240
and to make you realize that really the importance of studying openings.

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:12.000
This is going to be one of the key takeaways from this little analysis session,

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:15.040
because you can really see that making general statements

00:38:15.040 --> 00:38:18.640
about what's good and what's not in the opening is possible to a degree.

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:20.880
I mean, you know, the developing your pieces is good,

00:38:20.880 --> 00:38:26.240
pushing pawns from the corners is bad, but even that isn't always true.

00:38:26.240 --> 00:38:28.800
One thing that's true with the bishop on c4

00:38:28.800 --> 00:38:31.840
can be false with the bishop on b5, and vice versa.

00:38:31.840 --> 00:38:35.600
But you definitely should not push your f-pawn with black

00:38:35.600 --> 00:38:39.840
unless you're specifically aware that it's a legitimate opening

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:40.880
that you've studied the theory.

00:38:42.160 --> 00:38:45.360
So anyways, I think bishop e5 here is very strong,

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:47.840
and if black trades on f5, then white gets the bishop pair,

00:38:47.840 --> 00:38:49.600
as well as an extra pawn.

00:38:49.600 --> 00:38:52.160
But at least here, black is able to castle.

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:55.120
At least here, black is able to bring his bishop out

00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:57.840
and to castle short, and the game continues.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:00.800
This e6 square, by the way, could be used as an outpost,

00:39:00.800 --> 00:39:03.280
potentially for white's rook, so I like rook e1 here.

00:39:03.280 --> 00:39:06.160
The engine gives about plus 1.5,

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:07.840
which I think is a reasonable assessment.

00:39:08.800 --> 00:39:11.520
Anyways, I know that was a lot.

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:13.440
I think that our opponents move fe.

00:39:13.440 --> 00:39:15.520
This is obviously the decisive mistake.

00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:18.880
And after knight c6 and queen h5, the game basically ends.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:21.440
Again, how did I see that quickly?

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:25.600
Well, I'm aware of this idea, queen h5, queen e5, from the Damiano.

00:39:26.240 --> 00:39:30.480
And of course, we're also aware that you can take on c6 whenever you want to,

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:33.440
and that relinquishes black's control over the e5 square.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:37.600
So f takes e4 is the losing move, and knight takes c6 is the way to punish it.

00:39:37.600 --> 00:39:40.800
Notice that we didn't just automatically grab on e4.

00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:45.200
As a matter of fact, knight takes e4, believe it or not, is a losing move.

00:39:45.200 --> 00:39:47.600
Knight c3 takes e4 loses the game.

00:39:47.600 --> 00:39:49.920
Maybe this is what our opponent was hoping for.

00:39:49.920 --> 00:39:52.960
Who can tell me why knight takes e4 loses material?

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:55.600
And you should see this in just a couple of seconds,

00:39:55.600 --> 00:39:58.480
because there's tension between one pair of knights.

00:39:58.480 --> 00:40:02.320
That means the white queen is tied down to the d4 knight,

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:05.120
and the simple queen e7 wins a piece.

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:09.840
Because the standard response doesn't work anymore, you lose the knight.

00:40:10.720 --> 00:40:14.080
And if you play f3, you run into d5, and you lose the piece that way.

00:40:14.080 --> 00:40:18.000
You can try bishop g5, but black can play knight f6.

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.480
And ultimately, you're going to lose the knight e4.

00:40:21.120 --> 00:40:24.960
So this shows you when you're facing an unorthodox opening,

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:27.680
an opening that you think is unsound.

00:40:27.680 --> 00:40:32.720
The first step is to kind of generally try to tell yourself why you think is unsound.

00:40:32.720 --> 00:40:35.200
What exactly is its main drawback?

00:40:35.200 --> 00:40:38.480
In this case, by specifically identifying its drawback,

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:41.600
which is that it, A, weakens this diagonal,

00:40:41.600 --> 00:40:44.960
and B, after bishop c4 weakens this other diagonal,

00:40:44.960 --> 00:40:49.680
I was able to more efficiently zero in on the best way to refute this opening.

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:51.440
First, we know that we want to open up the center.

00:40:51.440 --> 00:40:56.160
And then second, we know that queen h5, queen e5 is often the way to punish

00:40:56.160 --> 00:40:59.200
openings that involve an early f5 or f6.

00:40:59.840 --> 00:41:04.160
Okay, so once again, I think knight to f6 was black's,

00:41:04.160 --> 00:41:07.520
really black's only chance to stay in the game.

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:13.280
Of course, knight takes d4 was probably also better than what our opponent did in the game.

00:41:13.280 --> 00:41:17.040
Maybe queen f6 here is an attempt to exchange queens.

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:18.400
But of course, we wouldn't have agreed.

00:41:18.400 --> 00:41:20.960
We would have played e5, driving the pawn in,

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:23.280
preventing black's knight from developing to f6.

00:41:23.920 --> 00:41:25.760
And this is just a hot mess.

00:41:25.760 --> 00:41:27.600
I mean, knight to f5, hitting the pawn.

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:29.520
And you can kind of dig around here on your own.

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:32.720
I think this will teach you a lot about how to play against such openings,

00:41:32.720 --> 00:41:34.720
but I don't want to bog you down too much.

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:37.760
Let's take a quick look at how the game proceeded.

00:41:37.760 --> 00:41:39.520
So knight c6, dc.

00:41:39.520 --> 00:41:41.520
I don't think it even matters which way black takes.

00:41:41.520 --> 00:41:44.000
The variations are very similar.

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:48.160
There is just one critical tactical detail

00:41:48.160 --> 00:41:51.680
that I had to foresee when I was making this move.

00:41:51.680 --> 00:41:52.960
This is really, really cool.

00:41:52.960 --> 00:41:58.880
So who can tell me a sequence of moves that seems to win the game almost on the spot,

00:41:58.880 --> 00:41:59.840
a la Damiano?

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:02.960
Like, this is the same sequence that we had in the Damiano,

00:42:02.960 --> 00:42:04.960
with one critical exception.

00:42:04.960 --> 00:42:07.440
The black pawn is now on c6 rather than b7.

00:42:08.240 --> 00:42:09.520
So what comes to mind here?

00:42:10.160 --> 00:42:13.120
Yeah, the best move is knight d5 check, which is ridiculous.

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:16.000
But how do we get to knight d5 check?

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:17.840
Well, first you should see queen e5 check.

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:20.880
Again, folks, bishop g5 does not win the queen.

00:42:20.880 --> 00:42:24.240
If you're newer to chess, don't assume that every skewer wins.

00:42:24.240 --> 00:42:26.560
But you can block a check.

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:29.040
Here, black blocks the check with knight f6.

00:42:29.040 --> 00:42:31.360
And white is still winning, I think, after knight e4.

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:36.800
But queen e5, king f7, and bishop c4 is exactly how we proceed in the Damiano.

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:38.720
King g6 gets checkmated.

00:42:38.720 --> 00:42:42.640
It's actually mate in three after queen takes e4, okay?

00:42:43.280 --> 00:42:44.800
But black has the move d5.

00:42:45.520 --> 00:42:49.040
But hopefully you're able to see that the king and rook are on forkable squares,

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:52.240
and white has the very straightforward knight takes d5.

00:42:52.240 --> 00:42:54.080
But here is the twist.

00:42:54.080 --> 00:42:57.920
If black plays cd, then bishop takes d5 just ends the game.

00:42:57.920 --> 00:43:01.440
It's actually not even about the rook so much that it is about the fact

00:43:01.440 --> 00:43:02.960
that white's king is checkmated.

00:43:02.960 --> 00:43:04.800
The engine gives mate in nine here.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:06.960
That starts with queen g3 check.

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:11.040
But clearly, wherever the king goes, it's going to get checkmated one way or the other.

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:14.960
Worst comes to worst, you could just take the rook and be up a ton of material.

00:43:15.760 --> 00:43:17.440
But what's the problem here, right?

00:43:17.440 --> 00:43:20.160
Again, you can never assume in these sharp positions

00:43:20.160 --> 00:43:22.720
that your opponent has to recapture a piece.

00:43:22.720 --> 00:43:25.600
You have to look more concretely than that.

00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:29.040
And if you do, you'll find that black has this weird move, bishop e6,

00:43:29.600 --> 00:43:33.760
getting the bishop out, and somehow white's pieces in the center are kind of loose.

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:36.480
You really don't want to be playing the move knight e3,

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:39.120
because this gives black a breather,

00:43:39.120 --> 00:43:43.440
and black can use this breather to quickly get the kingside pieces out

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:44.960
and complete the manual castle.

00:43:44.960 --> 00:43:47.840
So anytime you have a flaw like this in a sequence,

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:50.880
you need to consider that same sequence with a different move order.

00:43:50.880 --> 00:43:52.800
This is an incredibly powerful tool.

00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:57.680
Switching the move order can eliminate various defensive resources,

00:43:57.680 --> 00:44:01.200
and that's why we come up with the immediate knight d5 trick.

00:44:01.200 --> 00:44:06.960
Same exact theme, but it does not allow black to cover the check with d5.

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:08.320
c takes d5 is forced.

00:44:09.040 --> 00:44:12.800
Now we deliver a check like this, a check like this.

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:15.120
This, and of course we don't have our bishop out,

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:18.480
so we will have to satisfy ourselves with queen takes rook,

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:20.720
but this is obviously completely winning.

00:44:20.720 --> 00:44:25.120
Don't take on e4 first because of queen e7 pinning the queens.

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:28.720
Anytime your king is in the center and a queen is on the same file,

00:44:28.720 --> 00:44:30.320
you have to be very careful.

00:44:30.320 --> 00:44:32.400
Knight d5 is absolutely beautiful.

00:44:32.400 --> 00:44:34.160
Yeah, knight d5 check is the move.

00:44:34.160 --> 00:44:38.400
But hopefully the logic is understandable now that I traced it.

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:39.840
We start with queen e5.

00:44:39.840 --> 00:44:44.000
We see that detail, bishop e6, and we switch up the move order starting with knight d5.

00:44:44.800 --> 00:44:47.760
Okay, so yeah, bc was more resilient.

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:52.480
Of course, here it's a different situation because black's light squared bishop is open,

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:57.280
but on the flip side, the light squares around black's king are even weaker

00:44:57.280 --> 00:44:59.760
with the d7 pawn gone to c6.

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:01.520
So again, if you look at the computer,

00:45:01.520 --> 00:45:06.000
there will be probably even four or five different ways to reach a decisive advantage.

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:09.760
I think the path that we chose is simple and incredibly strong.

00:45:09.760 --> 00:45:11.360
It's the second move according to the engine,

00:45:11.920 --> 00:45:14.320
and I don't want to get too bogged down in the details here.

00:45:14.320 --> 00:45:16.720
It's very obvious that black is getting checkmated.

00:45:16.720 --> 00:45:19.200
Again, g6, queen e5 is just made in two.

00:45:20.080 --> 00:45:21.200
We don't even need to win the rook.

00:45:21.760 --> 00:45:26.240
Knight f6 does not defend against queen f7, and I'll let you figure out the rest.

00:45:27.040 --> 00:45:28.640
What else could black have done?

00:45:28.640 --> 00:45:34.960
I was expecting queen e8 originally, and here I was intending…

00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:36.960
Well, I thought there were two good moves.

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:41.840
The first I thought was bishop g5 check, knight f6, bishop takes f6,

00:45:41.840 --> 00:45:46.400
and then swing the queen over to c5, forcing the king onto the d file,

00:45:46.400 --> 00:45:48.000
and now you castle with check.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:51.200
I thought this was a decisive attack, but now that I look at it,

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:52.720
maybe this is not as convincing.

00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:59.120
According to the engine, on queen e8, queen e5 check is best, king d7.

00:46:00.080 --> 00:46:03.280
Now you give another diagonal check.

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:04.880
Okay, king drops back.

00:46:04.880 --> 00:46:06.720
Now you develop the bishop.

00:46:06.720 --> 00:46:11.200
Okay, and now whatever black does, you castle with check, and this is just absolutely…

00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:13.200
Oh, look at this line.

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:17.120
Takes, takes, and bang, using the pin on the bishop,

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:20.720
and now in comes the second rook with mate to follow.

00:46:21.360 --> 00:46:22.720
Yeah, so many cool lines.

00:46:22.720 --> 00:46:24.320
When you've got this many pieces in the attack,

00:46:24.320 --> 00:46:28.400
you're always going to get, you know, really nice tactical details.

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:29.600
Simple, but very nice.

00:46:30.320 --> 00:46:32.720
So in this position, black has to step the other way,

00:46:32.720 --> 00:46:34.880
but here you still develop the bishop with check,

00:46:36.000 --> 00:46:41.520
and here you deliver another check, forcing the king to the d file.

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:45.760
And according to the engine, you're supposed to take on f6 twice,

00:46:45.760 --> 00:46:50.960
attack the rook, castle queenside, and I can go on and on here,

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:53.040
but I'll just show one more cool detail.

00:46:53.040 --> 00:46:57.680
After rook f8, white has a really beautiful sequence that wins an exchange,

00:46:57.680 --> 00:47:00.320
and of course, with the exchange, black's whole position crumbles.

00:47:00.320 --> 00:47:06.080
Who can spot this 2-mover along the same theme

00:47:06.080 --> 00:47:08.000
that the combination we just saw occurred?

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:12.160
Yeah, bishop e6 check, deflection, and queen takes rook,

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:13.360
using the pin on the bishop.

00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:18.240
So, so, so many of these lines all around.

00:47:19.040 --> 00:47:21.120
Yeah, so maybe queen e8 was more resilient.

00:47:21.120 --> 00:47:25.200
Bishop e6, we take, we take, and the game is over.

00:47:25.200 --> 00:47:28.560
The engine gives around plus 5, which I think is an accurate assessment,

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:31.360
and it keeps growing the more it looks at this position.

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:35.680
Queen d5, I think, is as good of a defensive attempt as any,

00:47:35.680 --> 00:47:37.280
forcing us to deliver this check,

00:47:37.840 --> 00:47:41.040
and then I think bishop d2 is really the star of the show.

00:47:41.040 --> 00:47:45.040
Also winning would have been the simple castles kingside,

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:48.080
but after h6, I wasn't actually all that convinced,

00:47:48.080 --> 00:47:50.880
and I didn't want to give black the opportunity to take this knight

00:47:50.880 --> 00:47:53.200
and sack the exchange and prolong the game.

00:47:54.080 --> 00:47:56.640
So bishop d2 is really the cherry on top.

00:47:56.640 --> 00:48:00.640
It's more important to involve the bishop than it is to involve the rook,

00:48:00.640 --> 00:48:04.000
and we note that the e8 square is defended by the queen.

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:05.200
That's very, very important.

00:48:05.760 --> 00:48:08.160
Bishop d2 is a quiet but deadly move.

00:48:08.160 --> 00:48:13.600
Black cannot stop bishop c3, and once again, b3 is the flashier execution,

00:48:13.600 --> 00:48:16.480
but the inferior execution because of the check on b4.

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:19.440
And the moment black sticks the bishop on c3,

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:22.320
you've actually lost all of your advantage here with white.

00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:26.720
Also, you have to be very careful about the d1 square being undefended.

00:48:27.760 --> 00:48:32.720
So in any case, we play bishop d2 to preclude that possibility.

00:48:33.360 --> 00:48:35.040
Then we stick the bishop on c3.

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:37.040
We give a check on e2.

00:48:37.040 --> 00:48:37.760
We go rook d1.

00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:40.080
And if you look at, again, if you flip on the computer,

00:48:40.080 --> 00:48:43.840
the best move is in fact bishop e5, which is what I pointed out.

00:48:43.840 --> 00:48:48.160
But this is where simple execution is just much more practical.

00:48:48.160 --> 00:48:51.520
Just take on h8, and you know for a fact that you can convert this position

00:48:51.520 --> 00:48:53.600
because black has no way of trapping the bishop.

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:55.120
There's no pawn to put on f6.

00:48:55.760 --> 00:48:58.800
And you know, I appreciate that our opponent didn't

00:48:58.800 --> 00:49:00.880
prolong the torture and resigned in this position.

00:49:00.880 --> 00:49:02.000
So only 18 moves.

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:05.840
Conceptually, a very simple game which illustrates the importance of

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:10.080
blasting open the center when you have a big development advantage,

00:49:10.080 --> 00:49:11.840
when your opponent has a weak king.

00:49:11.840 --> 00:49:17.120
But hopefully, you also found the sort of theoretical exposition to be helpful.

00:49:17.120 --> 00:49:20.560
I will be doing a video in the near future on this opening,

00:49:20.560 --> 00:49:23.040
which I think is called the Rousseau Gambit.

00:49:23.040 --> 00:49:25.120
And it is problematic for a lot of players.

00:49:25.120 --> 00:49:26.560
And there's quite a bit of theory here.

00:49:26.560 --> 00:49:28.480
I have some cool analysis in this opening.

00:49:29.280 --> 00:49:33.360
And the refutation starts with d4, e takes d4,

00:49:33.360 --> 00:49:35.360
and now actually the move e5,

00:49:35.360 --> 00:49:38.320
driving the pawn in and preventing the knight from developing.

00:49:38.320 --> 00:49:41.280
But there's a lot of stuff here to break down.

00:49:41.840 --> 00:49:43.680
So I'll be doing that pretty shortly.

00:49:44.880 --> 00:49:47.760
f5 against the Ruy Lopez is a totally legitimate line.

00:49:47.760 --> 00:49:51.680
I played it against Dominguez in the U.S. Championship in the last year,

00:49:51.680 --> 00:49:52.720
and I managed to make a draw.

00:49:52.720 --> 00:49:57.040
So you can definitely consider the Schliemann as a very interesting way

00:49:57.040 --> 00:49:59.440
to spice things up against the Ruy Lopez.

00:49:59.440 --> 00:50:01.200
But on that note, folks, I think we'll call it a day.

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:02.000
It's really late.

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:02.800
I'm going to go to sleep.

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:04.400
I'll see you guys later.

00:50:04.400 --> 00:50:05.280
Have a good night, everybody.

00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:18.640
Thanks again.

